  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\operatorname{Countable}\\{z \\in \\mathbb{C} : z \\text{ is algebraic over } \\mathbb{Q}\\}",
    "plain": "Show that the algebraic complex numbers are countable by enumerating integer polynomials and using the finiteness of the root set of each nonzero polynomial.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "The result is the countability half of the standard proof that transcendental numbers exist.",
      "This OQ version emphasizes a constructive Lean route that avoids choosing canonical roots."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "The `Countable (Polynomial Z)` instance provides the enumeration base for integer polynomials.",
      "`nonzero_polys_countable` proves that the nonzero integer polynomials form a countable subtype.",
      "`finitely_many_roots` proves each nonzero integer polynomial has finitely many complex roots.",
      "`IsAlgebraic` packages algebraic complex numbers as roots of nonzero integer polynomials.",
      "`algebraic_numbers_countable` proves the algebraic numbers are a countable union of finite root sets.",
      "`algebraic_countable_type` transfers the result to the subtype of algebraic complex numbers."
    ],
    "open": [
      "A full `Denumerable` bijection/enumerator for the algebraic-number subtype is discussed but not constructed here."
    ],
    "goal": "Keep the zero-axiom countability proof available as a compact constructive bridge from polynomials to algebraic numbers."
  },
